module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.type_variable_means_both_declared_generic_and_inference_variable

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef, WholeDeclaration }

data type_variable_means_both_declared_generic_and_inference_variable: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "type_variable_means_both_declared_generic_and_inference_variable" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "TypeVariable means both declared generic and inference variable. INVALID STATE: one name, `v1.std.core` InferredNode `TypeVariable`, carries TWO materially different facts -- (1) a DECLARED GENERIC TYPE PARAMETER, inserted into the environment by v1.compiler.infer_env env_with_type_variable_bindings, and (2) an UNRESOLVED INFERENCE VARIABLE standing for a value whose type is not yet pinned. HARM: a consumer that asks a binding whether its inferred is a TypeVariable cannot tell which fact it just learned, and the two have opposite consequences. This is the DESIGN section 3 meaning fork -- one name, two meanings -- inside the seed's own vocabulary.",

    "RECEIPT, 2026-09-20, session gentle-seal-490. A new floor wall refusing a TYPE PARAMETER in value position keyed on `is_type_variable(binding.resolved.inferred)`. Because a lambda binder carries meaning (2) mid-inference, the wall refused 24 ORDINARY VALUE BINDERS across 11 corpus modules on CI: `e` in a fold lambda (gunbc.fabric.fabric_cell_effect x4), `item` in a value binder (gunbc.host.host_converge x2), plus evidence x3, s x2, p x2, and key, first, report, o, paths, path, line, seg, text, t. The wall intended to close a silent-wrongness class committed that class itself.",

    "THE DISCRIMINATOR ALREADY EXISTED AND IS NOT A SPLIT OF THE FORK. v1.compiler.infer_resolve fn_type_param_names is the authority for a declaration's DECLARED type-parameter names -- it is what env_with_type_variable_bindings is handed in the first place. Asking whether a name is in the ENCLOSING DECLARATION's roster is identity-keyed against a declared list and cannot be satisfied by a binder's mid-inference provenance. The roster is carried to the check site on v1.compiler.infer InferScope, which already holds locals, body_locals, match_bound_names, lambda_param_provenance and in_flight_lambda_param_names -- the same family of scope-bound naming facts.",

    "THE FIX ROUTES AROUND THE FORK AND DOES NOT CLOSE IT, stated so this row is not read as retired. TypeVariable still carries both meanings after the repair; what changed is that one consumer stopped asking it the ambiguous question. Every other consumer that asks a TypeVariable what it means is still exposed, and this row stays open until the two facts are separately representable.",

    "REJECTED REPAIRS, recorded so they are not re-walked. Keying on SPELLING -- the 24 refused names are lowercase while this corpus's declared generics are N, T, R, C, U -- is a stringly proxy that fails silently the day a declaration writes `fn f<k>`. Keying on v1.compiler.infer InferScope caller_decl_name is a fail-open: that field is set from a caller's decl_name in one arm and to the literal `<unknown>` in another, so a wall keyed on it silently admits everything reaching the second arm.",

    "RUNG FOUND AT: mitigatable -- the ambiguity is live and consumers must each avoid it by construction. ATTAINABLE CEILING: structurally impossible, by giving the two facts separate constructors so a consumer cannot ask one question and receive the other. NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER: InferredNode distinguishes a declared generic parameter from an unresolved inference variable -- SUFFICIENT FOR no consumer to be able to conflate them, so the discriminating question is unwritable rather than merely avoided.",
  ],

  evidence: [
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "v1.compiler.infer_env", decl_name: "env_with_type_variable_bindings", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "v1.compiler.infer_resolve", decl_name: "fn_type_param_names", field: WholeDeclaration },
  ],
}
